	doi = {10.1037/0882-7974.8.1.59},
	abstract = {Investigated the extent to which age had an impact on the performance of computer-based work. Three simulated real-world computer-interactive tasks that varied in complexity and pacing requirements were evaluated. Ss included 65 women, ranging in age from 25 to 70 yrs. The methodology encompassed physiological, subjective, and objective performance measures. Data indicated that previous computer experience and age had a significant impact on the performance of the 3 tasks. Increased age was associated with longer response times and a greater number of errors for all 3 tasks. Age also influenced perceptions of fatigue and task difficulty. The findings are discussed in terms of the implications for training and job design. ({PsycInfo} Database Record (c) 2025 {APA}, all rights reserved)},
